<section>
  <exercises>
    <heading level="1">11.10. Exercises</heading>
    <exercise number="1" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>If $$f(x) = \sum_{n = 0}^{\infty}{b_{n}\left( {x - 5} \right)^{n}}$$ for all $$x$$ , write a formula for $$b_{8}$$ .</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$b_{8} = f^{(8)}(5)/8!$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="2" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>The graph of $$f$$ is shown.</paragraph>
        <figure>
          <image alt="Details A curve is graphed on the x y coordinate plane. Both the axes are displayed from 0 to 4 in increments of 1. The curve enters from the bottom left, crosses the y axis at approximately (0, 0,5), goes up to the right, initially concave up and then concave down, reaches a high point at approximately (2.5, 3.9), goes down to the right, initially concave down and then concave up, running almost parallel to the x-axis as it exits at approximately (5.5, 1.1)."/>
        </figure>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>Explain why the series $$1.1 + 0.7x^{2} + 2.2x^{3} + \cdots$$ is not the Maclaurin series of $$f$$ .</item>
          <item>Explain why the series $$1.6 - 0.8\left( {x - 1} \right) + 0.4\left( {x - 1} \right)^{2} - 0.1\left( {x - 1} \right)^{3} + \cdots$$ is not the Taylor series of $$f$$ centered at $$1$$ .</item>
          <item>Explain why the series $$2.8 + 0.5\left( {x - 2} \right) + 1.5\left( {x - 2} \right)^{2} - 0.1\left( {x - 2} \right)^{3} + \cdots$$ is not the Taylor series of $$f$$ centered at $$2$$ .</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="3" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>If $$f^{(n)}(0) = \left( {n + 1} \right)!$$ for $$n = 0,1,2,\ldots$$ , find the Maclaurin series for $$f$$ and its radius of convergence.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$\sum\limits_{n = 0}^{\infty}{\left( {n + 1} \right)x^{n}}$$ , $$R = 1$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="4" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Find the Taylor series for $$f$$ centered at $$4$$ if</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$f^{(n)}(4) = \frac{\left( {- 1} \right)^{n}n!}{3^{n}\left( {n + 1} \right)}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>What is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series?</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <instruction>5, 6, 7, 8, 9, and 10 Use the definition of a Taylor series to find the first four nonzero terms of the series for $$f(x)$$ centered at the given value of $$a$$ .</instruction>
